# demo-clean — pass fixture for .formwork/rules/todo-fixme-hack-shell.yaml.
#
# Nothing here is an annotation marker IN SHELL. Every line is a shape a
# lexer-free comment-opener regex gets wrong in one direction or the other, so
# each one is load-bearing: this fixture fails the moment the rule loses its
# preprocessor or its marker-boundary rules.
#
# Two lines below pin the PREPROCESSOR specifically — they carry a marker
# after a space-preceded `#`, so the marker-boundary rules alone do not
# suppress them and only `preprocess: destring-sh` can. One sits in a
# double-quoted string, one in a heredoc body, which are the two blanking
# mechanisms the rule's comment claims. Without them the preprocessor could be
# deleted with every fixture still green (the whole-tree scan stays clean too),
# which is exactly the state this fixture was in when it was written.
set -euo pipefail

# See issue #9272; the retired gate was scripts/check-todo-fixme-hack.sh — a
# marker in the INTERIOR of a compound token is not an annotation, because a
# `.`/`-`/`/` sits on BOTH sides of it: pkg/todo/x, chromedriver-e2e.XXXXXX.log.
# A word character glues just as well, on either side: todoList, todo_list,
# hackathon, 0xXXXf, and the Go convention name `func TestXxx`.
#
# A marker at the HEAD of a word has no such glue and IS an annotation, so
# naming a file whose name starts with one means writing its path — which is
# what the first line of this comment does, and what every such reference in
# the repo already does. fire-2 carries the head-glued shapes.

# A gate's own pattern table is string DATA, not a comment.
PAT='# TODO: annotation marker'
MSG="a HACK named inside a double-quoted message"
HELP="run the gate, then grep the tree for # FIXME in comments"

# A heredoc body is template DATA. The emitted .sql is scanned as .sql by the
# sibling rule, which is where a forgotten placeholder gets caught.
cat > out.sql <<'SQLTPL'
-- TODO: describe the schema change and WHY (cite the issue/PR).
SQLTPL

# A heredoc that emits SHELL. The `#` below opens no comment in THIS file, so
# destring-sh must blank it and this rule must stay silent — that is the pin.
# It is not unread: it is exactly what the -embedded sibling in that same
# rule file reads, over the complementary projection, and it is a FIRE case
# there. The two rules meet on this line and disagree by design.
cat > out.sh <<'SHTPL'
# TODO: fill in the generated step
SHTPL

# `#` opens a comment only at a word start; these are parameter syntax.
n=${#arr[@]}
b=${0##*/}
printf '%s %s %s %s\n' "${n}" "${b}" "$#" "${PAT}"
printf '%s %s\n' "${MSG}" "${HELP}"
